{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine a world where humanity masters \nevery planetary resource available to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8520,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it—our first step on the famous Kardeshev \nscale of technological advancement. How  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13760,
    "dDurationMs": 14480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distant is that step? Will we even become a true \nType-1 civilization, and how can we get there? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1964  Soviet physicist Nikolai Kardashev \nproposed a method for measuring the technological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advancement of a civilization. The Kardashev scale \nhas become a go-to metric for what technologically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39840,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advanced civilization might be capable of. It’s \nbased on amount of energy commanded —with a Type  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48080,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I Civilisation controlling access to all available \nenergy on a planetary scale, a Type 2 accessing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54200,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an entire star’s energy output, and a Type 3 \nharnessing energy from a galactic scale. Fun side  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "note: Kardashev originally came up with this scale \nin a paper discussing the detectability of alien  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67840,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations, because the distance over which an \nalien broadcast might be detectable depends on how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much energy is pumped into that transmission.\nDespite this modest intended application,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the whole idea of the Kardashev scale really \ncaptured the imagination of both scientists and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84880,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science fiction-ists. Exponential advancement on a \ncosmological timescale implies frighteningly vast  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "differences between between civilizations born at \ndifferent times, and Kardeshev captures that. Type  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99720,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2 and 3 civilizations—mastery of one or many stars \nrespectively—are the stuff of extreme sci-fi—we’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106560,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talking Dyson spheres, black hole engines, \nartificial worlds, galactic empires, and warping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fabric of space for fun. By comparison, \nthe Type 1 civilization is just getting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117120,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started. Maybe it’s traveled to the nearest star \nsystems and terraformed a planet or two at best. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123400,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Humans would be Type 0. Maybe 0.5 at best. But if \nwe hope to one day become a super-civilisation;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131080,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "roaming the galaxy, mastering the \nfundamental constituents of reality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and finally making contact with the aliens who \nhave been waiting for us to graduate technology  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140400,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preschool… we need to start somewhere. And that \nmeans becoming a Type I civilisation. So what’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145960,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it going to take for us to get there.\nThere’s a lot more to becoming a stable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super-civilization than energy \nconsumption—including some messy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geopolitical stuff that I’m neither qualified nor \nhave time to cover in a short YouTube video—so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160400,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "today we’re going to stick to Kardeshev’s \nsimple criterion and ask what will it take to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165480,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attain the defining energy resources of a Type I.\nIn that 1964 paper, Kardeshev actually says that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172840,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humanity is close to the technological level of \na Type 1 civilization and defines a Type 1 power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capacity at roughly the current level. But he was \nspeaking quite loosely, and these days we tend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184480,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to think of Type I as already having access to \nplanetary-scale energy resources—which is a factor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 10,000 times higher than what we currently use. \nSo that’s the thought experiment for today—what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does it take to master planet-scale energy.\nLet’s attach a number to that statement. How  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202560,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much energy can we access from the safety of \nour home planet? We want a stable civilization,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209320,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we’re just going to consider renewable \nsources. Besides, a proper Type 1 would burn  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215520,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of the energy locked up in Earth’s \nfossil fuels and accessible fissionable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "material like uranium in a couple of days.\nRenewable sources like wind and waves contain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226040,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of energy, but the amount is piddling \ncompared to the amount of solar energy that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230800,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hits the Earth. And that makes sense—ultimately, \nalmost every source of energy in the atmosphere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or biosphere comes from the Sun. So we actually \nhave a very easy way to estimate the energy use  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a Type-1 civilization—it’s the amount of solar \nenergy hitting the entire planet every day. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248800,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directly collecting solar energy may also be the \neasiest way to access that power. So a sun-powered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255240,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Type-1 will be our first consideration. There is \nanother way we could become Type-1 that may yield  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262480,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even more energy, but I’ll come back to that.\nThe average solar irradiance at the Earth is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269840,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1361 W/m^2, and our planet intercepts that \nenergy over a disk with the Earht’s radius,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278560,
    "dDurationMs": 8920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "giving us an upper limit of 1.7x10^17 Watts to \nplay with—let’s say 10^17 Watts. To put this into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287480,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perspective, we currently use around 10^13 watts \nglobally—10,000 times shy of our Type I goal. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Collecting anywhere close to 10^17 Watts in solar \npower is going to take a lot more than covering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300520,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet in modern solar panels—not that we’d \nwant to do that anyway. The first program in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upgrading our Kardeshev scale is improving our \nsolar collection capabilities. So let’s start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by learning a bit more about solar cells.\nThe most common solar cells are typically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317440,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made from silicon and are an application of \nsemiconductor technology that we discussed here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To summarize, you have two layers of silicon, \neach mixed or doped with a different element.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of these doped elements grants an extra \nfree electron to its layer—this is a p-type  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332760,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "senmiconductor and on its own those free electrons \nallow the flow of current. The other layer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doped element subtracts one electron, creating \nan electron hole and an n-type semiconductor. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345360,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And those holes can also flow as a current. \nNow if you splice those lakers together,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351880,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the free electrons diffuse from p-type to \nn-type—across the p-n junction—to fill the holes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358240,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at which point the system is non-conducting. But \nif you provide energy to those electrons they jump  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back across the p-n junction and are able to flow \naround any circuit that the device is attached to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369400,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is exactly how transistors work, \nwith the energy being provided by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small electric potential. In solar cells, \nthe energy is provided by incoming photons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a process called photoexcitation. In a \nsolar panel, a circuit provides a path for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those electrons to return to the n-type layer \nand their energy can be extracted en route. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389800,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a fundamental limit to how much \nenergy a solar cell can extract from light.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is called the Shockley-Queisser \nlimit. There are a few effects that go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398280,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into determining this limit, but for now let’s \nfocus on one of the big ones: spectrum losses. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404080,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The energy required to move an electron \nbetween layers is a very specific number:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408200,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "typically the energy of a near-infrared \nphoton. Photons with less energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413280,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means most infrared, do nothing \nat all. Photons with more energy—all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible and ultraviolet light—will excite their \nelectron, but the excess energy gets lost as heat. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The photons in sunlight come with a very broad \nrange of energies, so some loss is inevitable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with an efficiency limit of around 50%. Other \neffects drag the efficiency—Shockley–Queisser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438160,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limit—down to around 30%. In practice we've only \njust cracked 25% efficiency at the moment. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is for traditional solar cells with \na single p-n junction. Newer designs and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449960,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "materials offer the potential to exceed this \nlimit. For example, layered p-n junctions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454480,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tuned to different energy photons can capture \na wider spectral range without wasting energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Certain special materials like perovskites have \na similar advantage. Quantum dots are another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465640,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exciting possibility are quantum dots thanks to \ntheir tunable absorption spectrum. In general,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "advances in materials science and nano-fabrication \nare likely to boost us over 50% efficiency. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even more important than conversion efficiency \nis where we put these cells. Space-based solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power has a major energetic advantages. \nOur atmosphere blocks most UV light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in space those UV photons increase the \noverall available energy for solar cells by a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495680,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factor of 10. Also there’s no night-time in space, \nwhich adds another factor of two. And of course,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s nice that we don’t have to cover Earth in \nthe things, leaving more room for the humans or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our AI overlords to inhabit our Type I world.\nA major challenge with space-based solar is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "getting the energy back to Earth. The most viable \ncandidate is microwave power transmission. 2.45  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "GHz photons pass easily through our atmosphere. \nPulses of these would be collected by receiver  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antennae – called rectennas — which would \nconvert those photons back to electricity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe we finally build that space elevator \nto send people up and channel power down. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538600,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’re really looking at swarms of solar arrays \norbiting Earth or in co-orbit around the Sun or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at Earth’s Lagrange points. With the James Webb \nSpace Telescope, we’ve demonstrated that we’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548400,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to send large, fragile things into space. To \nachieve our 10^17 Watt power source we’ll need an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556040,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "array quite a bit larger than the flattened disk \nof the Earth in order to make up for the losses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561600,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which will never be zero. That’s more than a \nhundred trillion square kilometers a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar collectors, and if we scale down our silicon \nsolar cell thickness by a factor of 10 that’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575800,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still a few trillion kilograms, not including \nsupporting structure. Fortunately silicon is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582480,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the second most abundant element on Earth after \noxygen, so a few trillion kilograms is nothing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Add a bit of asteroid mining for the supporting \ninfrastructure, this feels distantly doable. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And of course this will put our new Type I \ncivilization on track to Type II — just keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building arrays until we wreath the entire \nSun and we have an effective Dyson sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602680,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solar is the most obvious path to Type-I-dom, \nbut it may not be the best. Remember that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun generates energy through nuclear fusion—by \nsmashing together hydrogen nuclei to make helium,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and converting a small fraction of the \nmass into energy. So rather than being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618800,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "content to soak up the energy that \nthe Sun happens to radiate our way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why not build our own mini-Suns on Earth? \nLet’s talk about artificial fusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630440,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Sun, particles are smashed \ntogether and fused into new,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633360,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavier nuclei in a series of reactions known \nas the proton-proton chain. It starts with lone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protons—hydrogen nuclei—and ends in Helium-4 \nnuclei. The most energy is obtained during a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645160,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particular step of that chain, when two types \nof heavy hydrogen—deuterium and tritium—smash  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650160,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together to form helium 4. About 0.4 of \n1% of the mass of the ingoing particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is converted into energy, mostly in the \nkinetic energy of an ejected neutron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663240,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That doesn’t sound very efficient, but remember, \nE=mc2 describes the conversion of mass to energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the c2 does some heavy lifting here. \nDeuterium-tritium fusion releases four times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676560,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as much energy as Uranium fission gram for \ngram. That means to reach our Type-I energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "goal of 10^17 Watts we’d need to convert \na few hundred kilograms of heavy hydrogen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into helium per second. Fortunately we have a \nmassive supply of deuterium—I’ll come back to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tritium. Earth’s oceans contain around \n1.4 billion billion metric tons of water,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698600,
    "dDurationMs": 9160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one eighth of which is hydrogen by mass, and 0.016 \npercent of that is hydrogen is deuterium. If we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707760,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "burned the oceans deuterium, we’d be able to power \nour Type 1 civilization for a few million years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before running out. And if we can’t make it to \nType 2 by then, well, maybe it wasn’t meant to be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Having the fuel for fusion is one thing, but we \nalso have to have the reactors. How long before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have that? Commercial fusion is famously 50 \nyears away no matter when you ask. That said,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731960,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are major programs to bring this \nto reality. The most promising approaches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735560,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "use powerful magnetic fields to \nconfine an extremely hot plasma,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trying to duplicate the crushing effect of \nthe intense gravity in the core of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most famous is the International \nThermonuclear Experimental reactor—ITER—in France;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a type of reactor called a Tokomak. There are \nother magnetic confinement projects and designs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also non-magnetic and non-confinement options, \nbut that’s not for this video. Currently magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confinement seems most promising for commercial \nreactors, and ITER is the furthest along."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768560,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are still many challenges to overcome \nbefore magnetic confinement fusion is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771960,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "commercially viable—from dealing the the plasma \ninstabilities to safely extracting the energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776880,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the extreme-energy neutrons produced in \nfusion. The main issue is getting more energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out that you put in to get the reaction started \nand maintained. And that seems to be a matter of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale. Larger reactors should have an easier time \ndoing that—and this is the great hope of ITER,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795520,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with its proposed 500 MegaWatt output \nsupposedly only needing 10% of that to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801480,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sustain the reaction. This is scheduled \nto come online in around 10 years or 50."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808320,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, back to the question. Assuming we get fusion \ntechnology, how many reactors do we need to power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our aspiring Type-I status? Let’s say we build \nmany ITERs. We only expect a fraction of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820200,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "output to be convertible to electricity—those \nneutrons are hard to catch. At 20% efficiency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825920,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d need 100,000 reactors to power our \nsuper-civilization. That is a lot, but it’s not …  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834960,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "utterly preposterous. If we double efficiency and \nscale reactor size by a factor of 10 then 5,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reactors scattered across the globe will provide \nevery Watt we’ll need for millions of years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847560,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with great power comes great need for \nstorage and distribution of that power.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the development of a planetary-scale \nsupergrid. Rather than stringing lossy copper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860040,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cables across the planet, we’re going \nto want to parallel-track development  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of high temperature superconducting cables. \nUnderground networks of, say, yttrium barium  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "copper oxide can distribute our new extreme \ncurrent density with minimal power dissipation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874920,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thanks to their zero resistance \nwhen they become superconducting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878960,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Challenges here include the fact that even \nhigh temperature superconductors need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be cooled quite a bit, which means both \nenergy and cumbersome piping. The energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888080,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "part we seem to have sorted, and we’re going \nto require those chunky pipes anyway due to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893800,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "typical brittleness of superconductors. This \nis stuff that companies are working on today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899520,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we can hope it’s sorted by the time we need it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943040,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have a couple of paths to \nType-1-civilization-hood. But now that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a completely ridiculous amount of energy, \nwhat do we do with it? Why did we go to all this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trouble anyway? Honestly, maybe we didn’t \nneed to. Maybe we can settle down and live  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 959120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in modest harmony with our planet and \nchill on these expansionist tendencies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe it’s juvenile to obsess over some made \nup metrics in a soviet paper from the 60s. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969960,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But seeing as we got this far, and we \nhave an imaginary Type-1 civilization,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we may as well do something with it. Part of \nbeing a Type I civilisation is the ability to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not just harness the energy on Earth, but to \nbe in control of its natural forces. We now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987200,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have access to energy on the same scale as the \natmosphere and the ocean, and so can, presumably,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992520,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "change them for the better. So I guess we start \nout by saving the planet, reverse climate change  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and all that, now that we actually plan \nto stick around as a species for a while. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They say if you have a hammer, every problem is \na nail. But energy is the most versatile hammer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008480,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is, and it’s the ultimate currency \nof problem-solving. Near limitless clean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy can enable colossal scales of resource \ngathering and manufacturing—from mass-scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019040,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "desalination and farming to asteroid mining, \nadvanced manufacturing, building perfect cities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024200,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for all peoples. We could turn the Earth into a \nparadise of abundance. Assuming we also solve all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030320,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world conflicts, which energy can’t do directly \nalthough surely eliminating scarcity helps."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energy is information and power is computation. \nOur supercomputers will run hot and grind out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043000,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solutions to all technological challenges and \nunlock the secrets of the universe with sheer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047760,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brute force processing and unthinkably capable \nAI. We’ll duplicate our energy facilities to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055080,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power the terraforming of other worlds, creating \nsimulacra of our Earthly paradise. And so humanity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will claw its way towards the next levels of \nKardashev’s scale. Or we might just spend the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067640,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy mining Kardeshev-coin and training our \nAI successors. But whether it’s us or them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074480,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth will be on track to spawning \na civilization of the Type II,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soon mastering the energy of an entire \nstar, and then of many. And then maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083720,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we look back at Nicholai Kardeshev’s little paper \nand realise that we are the beacons he imagined,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that we’ve announced our presence to new \nType-0s still emerging across galactic spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097760,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting PBS. \nScientists often find themselves in some of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102160,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the most remote corners of the world. And whether \nyou’re in a remote location or just working from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "home, the need for reliable, secure communication \nand data transfer remains important. With Anydesk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1113520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "file transfer functionality allows for \nfast, secure, and direct file transfers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117960,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bypassing the cloud entirely. With coverage all \nover the world Anydesk remains fast and reliable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1124000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with over 99% uptime. And when you’re \nworking on that groundbreaking research,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128680,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you don’t want any distractions. Thankfully \nAnydesk features a clean user interface and a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple user experience as well as compatibility \nacross multiple computing and mobile platforms.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1140400,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So even if your astronomical data \nset is still running on WindowsXP9,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anydesk still has you covered with \nbackwards compatibility. Anydesk is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150680,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "available for personal use, but if your rese\narch requires a lot of global coordination,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1155520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anydesk offers custom-tailored business \nplans. To learn more about Anydesk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1160200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go to anydesk.com/spacetime or check \nout the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165120,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey, Everyone! You know how black holes pull \neverything in with irresistible gravity?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you know how the upcoming holiday season often \nfeels the exact same way? Well that’s why we’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174440,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like to welcome you to our first ever Black Hole \nFriday Sale! On Friday, November 29th, for one day  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182360,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only the entire Merch store catalog will be on \nsale for 20% off. We also have a bunch of brand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1188960,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new merch such as the first ever Multifunctional \nSpace Time Pen, which features a level, rulers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1195520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in inches, centimeters, and millimeters, a stylus \nand micro screwdriver. It’s basically a low-grade  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201000,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sonic screwdriver yet. Stand by for the full-tech \nversion. If you need somewhere to use that pen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207360,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we have our first ever collection of Space Time \nPocket Logs which are perfect for capturing the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations for your theory of quantum gravity. We \nalso have a new Standard Model LED night light for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1217560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you’re working on your theories late into the \nevening, a new Neural Net desktop mat to keep your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1223680,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "computer stylish while your run your simulations, \na 520 Piece Puzzle for when you’re waiting on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1230520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those simulations to render, our first ever Poster \nthat will remind you of where all elements came  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1236640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from, and a Penrose Diagram Black Hole Rocks \nGlass, for when you need a tasty beverage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1243040,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to celebrate of your successful theory. Can we \nlegally say buying Space Time merch will help you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1249080,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formulate a Nobel Prize winning physics theory? \nThat’s a grey area. But we can offer you 20% off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1256120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every t-shirt, hoodie, patch, pin we’ve sold this \nyear during their special re-appearance on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1262040,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Merch store. We can also let you know about this \namazing Space Time Ugly Holiday Sweater, which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1263200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you could theoretically wear to holiday events \nor to accept any kind of important international  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1269680,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prize. And speaking of the non-Americans, if \nyou're one of our many international viewers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1275960,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "November 29th will be that last date we can \nguarantee international delivery before December  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1281840,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "25th. So if you need a gift for any special person \nin the galaxy, including yourself, remember to go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1289120,
    "dDurationMs": 29400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to PBSspacetime.com/shop on November 29th \nfor our 1st ever Black Hole Friday sale!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
